          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/substitution"} substitution
            is a meta-level notion, i.e. an operation
            %em on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntax
            %em explicit substitutions
            incorporate a notion of substitution as
            %em part
            of a syntax. The original calculus of explicit substitutions was introduced by
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#AbadiCardelliCurienLévy91"} Abadi, Cardelli, Curien &amp; Lévy 1991
          %h2#definitionexample Definition/Example
          %p
            A paradigmatic (and in some sense universal) example is given by adding explicit substitutions to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simply+typed+lambda+calculus"} simply typed lambda calculus
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M\langle x\coloneqq N\rangle
          %p
            denoting internally “the substitution of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            in
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\langle x\coloneqq y\rangle
            is a term distinct from
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            = succeed "," do
              %em operation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x[x\coloneqq y]
            would be a notation for the result of the operation of substituting the term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            for the variable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            in the term
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
          %p
            The relation between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\langle x\coloneqq y\rangle
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            is recovered by modifying the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduction"} reduction
            relation. We modify the usual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/beta-reduction"} beta-reduction
            rule
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\lambda x.M)(N) \to M[x\coloneqq N]
            to use an explict substitution instead:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\lambda x.M)(N) \to M\langle x\coloneqq N\rangle
          %p
            and then give reduction rules that mimic the
            %em inductive definition
            of the usual
            %em operation
            of substitution on terms:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                          %mi x
                          %mo ≔
                          %mi N
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                          %mi N
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                          %mi x
                          %mo ≔
                          %mi N
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                          %mi y
                          %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo ≠
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi M
                            %mn 1
                          %msub
                            %mi M
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                          %mi x
                          %mo ≔
                          %mi N
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi M
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                          %mi x
                          %mo ≔
                          %mi N
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi M
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                          %mi x
                          %mo ≔
                          %mi N
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi λ
                          %mi y
                          %mo .
                          %mi M
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                          %mi x
                          %mo ≔
                          %mi N
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                          %mi λ
                          %mi y
                          %mo .
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi M
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                          %mi x
                          %mo ≔
                          %mi N
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo ≠
                          %mi y
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mtext and
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi y
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mtext not free in
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi N
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  x\langle x\coloneqq N\rangle &amp;\to N\\
                  y \langle x\coloneqq N\rangle &amp;\to y \quad (x\neq y)\\
                  (M_1 M_2)\langle x\coloneqq N\rangle &amp;\to (M_1 \langle x\coloneqq N\rangle)(M_2 \langle x\coloneqq N\rangle) \\
                  (\lambda y.M)\langle x\coloneqq N\rangle &amp;\to \lambda y. (M\langle x\coloneqq N\rangle) \quad (x\neq y \;\text{and}\; y \;\text{not free in}\; N)
                  \\end{aligned}
          %p
            (We are being somewhat cavalier about
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} variables
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Bruijn+indices"} de Bruijn indices
          %h2#combining_substitutions_and_strong_normalization Combining substitutions and strong normalization
          %p
            Note that there is no rule for reducing a term of the form
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi y
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (M \langle x\coloneqq N\rangle) \langle y\coloneqq P\rangle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \langle x\coloneqq N\rangle
            until its “head” is no longer an explicit substitution, and then we can apply reduction rules for the “outer” substitution
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi y
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle y\coloneqq P\rangle
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi y
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi y
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi y
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (M \langle x\coloneqq N\rangle) \langle y\coloneqq P\rangle \to (M\langle y\coloneqq P\rangle) \langle x\coloneqq N\langle y\coloneqq P\rangle \rangle
          %p
            but such a rule is undesirable because it destroys the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+normalization"} strong normalization
            property of the reduction system: although every term still
            %em can
            be reduced to a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+form"} normal form
          %p One might hope that this could be avoided by introducing “explicit simultaneous substitutions” with a reduction rule
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi y
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi N
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (M \langle x\coloneqq N\rangle) \langle y\coloneqq P\rangle \to M \langle x\coloneqq N, y\coloneqq P\rangle.
          %p
            Apparently for a long time it was hoped that this would work, but
            %a{:href => "#Mellies"} Mellies
            showed that it doesn’t: explicit simultaneous substitutions with a rule of this sort also yield infinite reduction sequences. Note that Mellies worked with a de Bruijn version, so his rules look somewhat different; a version of the counterexample using named variables can be found in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BlooRose"} Bloo-Rose
          %h2#categorical_models_of_explicit_substitutions Categorical Models of Explicit Substitutions
          %p
            The original calculus of explicit substitutions of Abadi et al (usually called
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda\sigma
          %p
            This explicit substitutions construction is parametric in the kind of logic that the terms stand for. The Calculus of Constructions (
            %a{:href => "#Ritter"}> Ritter
            ), Intuitionistic Propositional Logic and Intuitionistic Linear Logic (
            %a{:href => "#Ghani2000"}> Ghani2000
            ) and propositional Constructive Necessity (
            %a{:href => "#Ghani1998"}> Ghani1998
            ) can be given formalizations and implementations in terms of explicit substitutions calculi. These constructions help to show the correctness of the implementation of the associated type theories.
          %h2#the_linear_substitution_calculus The linear substitution calculus
          %p
            In 2010 a new version of a
            %em linear substitution calculus
            was introduced by Delia Kesner and Beniamino Accattoli, which according to Damiano Mazza is based on two fundamental ideas:
          %blockquote
            %p
              %em>
                Explicit substitutions in the
                = succeed "-calculus" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi λ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
              \: if you write down a term calculus for the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo ⊸
                    %mo ,
                    %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\multimap,!\}
              fragment of ILL, however you do it, you’re going to see let binders pop up, and these behave very much like explicit substitutions, in the sense that normalization needs commuting conversions. Now, if you take Girard’s (call-by-name) embedding of the
              = succeed "-calculus" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi λ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
              = succeed "-reduction" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi β
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
              = succeed "-calculus," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi λ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
              = succeed "-calculus:" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi λ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            %p
              %em> Explicit substitutions should behave like boxes in proof nets
              \: the let binders mentioned above correspond to what are known as “exponential boxes” in proof nets. Cut-elimination in proof nets has the amazing property (from the rewriting viewpoint) that it does not need commuting conversions. In terms of explicit substitutions, this means that ESs should not need to “move” through a term in order to be applied, one should be able to apply them just where they are created. Rewriting should happen “remotely” or, as they say, “at a distance”. Beniamino and Delia noticed that there’s a consistent way of generalizing redexes in a term calculus with ESs (no need for proof nets!) so that they are transparent to the presence of ESs. Combine this with the above idea (that ESs should behave like in linear logic, i.e., be “linear”, substituting one occurrence at a time) and you obtain the LSC.
          %p The LSC, just as the previous versions of explicit substitutions calculi, has found applications in
          %blockquote
            %p
              %strong> abstract machines
              \: Beniamino Accattoli, Pablo Barenbaum, and Damiano Mazza showed how the LSC may be related in a very sharp way to a host of abstract machines, for any reduction strategy you can think of (call by name, call by value, call by need). Each strategy in the LSC “distills” an abstract machine, in the sense that the rewriting rules of the LSC are in bijection with the actual computational transitions of the machines, while the “administrative” transitions (needed by the machine to navigate through the term in search for the next redex) sort of “evaporate” and become structural equivalences in the LSC (they relate syntactically different terms which are behaviorally equal). In particular, the LSC gives a slick formulation of (weak) call-by-need.
          %p In fact the formalization of abstract machines is given as the original motivation of Abadi et al.
          %p The LSC also has applications in:
          %blockquote
            %p
              %strong> complexity
              \: for decades, people had been wondering whether
              = succeed "-reduction" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi β
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computational+complexity"} computational complexity
              = succeed "-reduction" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi β
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/explicit+parametricity"} explicit parametricity
          %h2#references References
          %ul#BlooRose
            %li{:id => "AbadiCardelliCurienLévy91"}
              %p
                Martín Abadi, Luca Cardelli,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre-Louis+Curien"} Pierre-Louis Curien
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Explicit Substitutions
                %a{:href => "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-functional-programming/article/explicit-substitutions/C1B1AFAE8F34C953C1B2DF3C2D4C2125"} link
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul-Andr%C3%A9+Melli%C3%A8s"}> Paul-André Melliès
                ,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    Typed
                    = succeed "-calculi" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi λ
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            %li
              %p
                Roel Bloo and Kristoffer H. Rose,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Preservation of Strong Normalisation in Named Lambda Calculi with Explicit Substitution and Garbage Collection
                %a{:href => "http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/summary?doi=10.1.1.51.5026"} citeseer
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Eike Ritter,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Categorical abstract machines for higher-order typed lambda calculi
                %a{:href => "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0304397594001253"} link
            %li
              %p
                Neil Ghani, Valeria de Paiva and Eike Ritter,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Linear Explicit Substitutions
                %a{:href => "https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Valeria-De-Paiva/publication/2503409_Linear_Explicit_Substitutions/links/0fcfd505c631c267d2000000/Linear-Explicit-Substitutions.pdf"} link
            %li
              %p
                Neil Ghani, Valeria de Paiva and Eike Ritter,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Explicit substitutions for constructive necessity
                %a{:href => "https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?repid=rep1&type=pdf&doi=712ec1a9241700486bb9aba5aba83f51398ec246"} citeseer
          %p The linear substitution calculus is discussed in:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Beniamino Accattoli,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An abstract factorization theorem for explicit substitutions
                %a{:href => "https://hal.archives-ouvertes.fr/hal-00780344"} link
            %li
              %p
                Beniamino Accattoli, Eduardo Bonelli, Delia Kesner, Carlos Lombardi,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A nonstandard standardization theorem
                %a{:href => "https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/2535838.2535886"} link
            %li
              %p
                Beniamino Accattoli, Pablo Barenbaum, Damiano Mazza,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Distilling Abstract Machines
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1406.2370"} arxiv
            %li
              %p
                Beniamino Accattoli and Ugo Dal Lago,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em (Leftmost-outermost) beta reduction is invariant, indeed
                %a{:href => "https://lmcs.episciences.org/1627/pdf"} lmcs
          %p
            Explicit substitution in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %span.newWikiWord>
                  Johan Georg Granström
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Johan+Georg+Granstr%C3%B6m"} ?
                \.
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Treatise on Intuitionistic Type Theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Th%C3%A9o+Winterhalter"}> Théo Winterhalter
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Formalisation and Meta-Theory of Type Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://github.com/TheoWinterhalter/phd-thesis/releases/download/v1.2.1/TheoWinterhalter-PhD-v1.2.1.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://github.com/TheoWinterhalter/phd-thesis"} github
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Gratzer"}> Daniel Gratzer
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G.+Alex+Kavvos"} G. Alex Kavvos
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andreas+Nuyts"} Andreas Nuyts
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lars+Birkedal"} Lars Birkedal
                = succeed "," do
